WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.733 --> 00:00:01.967
-Hi, everybody.

00:00:01.967 --> 00:00:04.003
Welcome to another
"Tonight Show: At Home Edition."

00:00:04.003 --> 00:00:06.139
I appreciate you watching
and staying at home

00:00:06.139 --> 00:00:07.440
and doing what we are told.

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:10.043
I know it's hard right now,
but it is gonna work out,

00:00:10.043 --> 00:00:11.878
so thank you for doing that.

00:00:11.878 --> 00:00:13.479
We're doing our part, too,
staying at home

00:00:13.479 --> 00:00:16.849
and washing our hands and not
touching our face, et cetera.

00:00:16.849 --> 00:00:21.320
And it's just -- Today is just
a Tuesday, I believe,

00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:22.522
and it's just another day.

00:00:22.522 --> 00:00:26.793
It's another week.
Every day is another week.

00:00:26.793 --> 00:00:29.161
So, here we go.
Let's make it easier.

00:00:29.161 --> 00:00:31.398
Let's start off
with a few jokes.

00:00:31.398 --> 00:00:35.034
It's time for the monologue.
♪♪

00:00:35.034 --> 00:00:36.535
Welcome to "The Tonight Show:
At Home Edition."

00:00:36.535 --> 00:00:38.204
Guys, there are reports that
Kim Jong-un

00:00:38.204 --> 00:00:41.240
is in serious condition
after having surgery.

00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:42.375
Wow. That's crazy.

00:00:42.375 --> 00:00:46.946
He always looked
so healthy to me.

00:00:46.946 --> 00:00:49.448
That's right -- there were
reports that Kim Jong-un

00:00:49.448 --> 00:00:52.218
is in serious condition
after having surgery.

00:00:52.218 --> 00:00:54.620
Who knew that the guy with the
body shaped like the one

00:00:54.620 --> 00:00:58.191
from "Operation"
would have health issues?

00:00:58.191 --> 00:00:59.659
He's got water on the knee.

00:00:59.659 --> 00:01:01.827
Bucket of water on the knee.

00:01:01.827 --> 00:01:03.295
If anything happens to
Kim Jong-un,

00:01:03.295 --> 00:01:05.097
his sister will take over.

00:01:05.097 --> 00:01:10.903
You know Kim's sisters --
Kourtney and Khloé Jong-un.

00:01:10.903 --> 00:01:12.237
Earlier today at
The White House,

00:01:12.237 --> 00:01:15.941
President Trump met with
New York governor Andrew Cuomo.

00:01:15.941 --> 00:01:17.210
It was strange,
because, normally,

00:01:17.210 --> 00:01:18.711
when two guys from Queens
meet up,

00:01:18.711 --> 00:01:22.214
it's under a bridge in
the middle of the night.

00:01:22.214 --> 00:01:25.318
The FDA just authorized
the first coronavirus test

00:01:25.318 --> 00:01:28.821
that allows patients to collect
their samples at home.

00:01:28.821 --> 00:01:31.424
That's right -- now people can
mail their sample in

00:01:31.424 --> 00:01:34.326
and then, two weeks later, get
a call from the lab that says,

00:01:34.326 --> 00:01:37.963
"Sir, that cup was for saliva."

00:01:37.963 --> 00:01:40.966
Down in Georgia,
a governor is going against

00:01:40.966 --> 00:01:42.368
medical experts
and reopening some

00:01:42.368 --> 00:01:45.804
non-essential businesses,
including bowling alleys.

00:01:45.804 --> 00:01:48.374
Look, even when
there's not a pandemic,

00:01:48.374 --> 00:01:50.476
I wouldn't be sticking
my fingers

00:01:50.476 --> 00:01:55.315
into a communal ball hole.

00:01:55.315 --> 00:01:57.049
Southern states
like Tennessee and Georgia

00:01:57.049 --> 00:01:58.718
are ignoring expert advise

00:01:58.718 --> 00:02:01.354
and reopening
non-essential businesses.

00:02:01.354 --> 00:02:03.222
That's cool, because the last
time the Southern states

00:02:03.222 --> 00:02:04.223
broke off
and did their own thing,

00:02:04.223 --> 00:02:07.360
it worked out pretty well,
right?

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:10.129
Georgia wants to reopen movie
theaters next week.

00:02:10.129 --> 00:02:12.932
It's perfect if you want to
catch the latest releases

00:02:12.932 --> 00:02:17.603
from February
and also the coronavirus.

00:02:17.603 --> 00:02:18.871
The World Series of Poker

00:02:18.871 --> 00:02:22.441
has been postponed
because of the coronavirus.

00:02:22.441 --> 00:02:25.244
Yep. Of course all the players
were incredibly upset,

00:02:25.244 --> 00:02:29.015
but it was impossible to tell.

00:02:29.015 --> 00:02:31.417
I read that seashells in
North Carolina beaches

00:02:31.417 --> 00:02:33.319
have been piling up
because no one is there

00:02:33.319 --> 00:02:35.255
to collect them,
the seashells, yeah.

00:02:35.255 --> 00:02:37.790
So now you know where to go when
you need another beach-themed

00:02:37.790 --> 00:02:42.729
"Live, laugh, love"
sign for your bathroom.

00:02:42.729 --> 00:02:44.897
A woman in Iowa made
hundreds of face masks

00:02:44.897 --> 00:02:46.399
and hung them on
a tree outside her house

00:02:46.399 --> 00:02:48.634
for people to share and take.

00:02:48.634 --> 00:02:50.836
That's really nice,
but while she was hanging them,

00:02:50.836 --> 00:02:52.905
you know the neighbors were
definitely like,

00:02:52.905 --> 00:02:57.109
"Susan's gone insane."

00:02:57.109 --> 00:02:59.078
And, finally, a lot of people
say that the lockdowns

00:02:59.078 --> 00:03:00.146
have created a lot of tension

00:03:00.146 --> 00:03:02.181
between them
and their roommates.

00:03:02.181 --> 00:03:03.983
A definite sign of that --
when you start referring

00:03:03.983 --> 00:03:06.852
to your wife and kids
as roommates.

00:03:06.852 --> 00:03:09.055
Guys, that is
our monologue right there.

00:03:09.055 --> 00:03:10.823
We have a great show tonight.

00:03:10.823 --> 00:03:13.225
Halle Berry is
on the show tonight.

00:03:13.225 --> 00:03:14.427
We love Halle Berry.

00:03:14.427 --> 00:03:15.661
We'll be talking to her
and all the things

00:03:15.661 --> 00:03:17.630
she's doing right now
during this quarantine.

00:03:17.630 --> 00:03:19.665
Also, Russell Westbrook and I

00:03:19.665 --> 00:03:21.767
talk about
Michael Jordan documentaries.

00:03:21.767 --> 00:03:23.536
We'll be talking about playoff.

00:03:23.536 --> 00:03:26.172
Is he ready for the playoffs if
NBA happens to come back?

00:03:26.172 --> 00:03:27.373
We don't know.

00:03:27.373 --> 00:03:29.241
And we have great music
from Meghan Trainor

00:03:29.241 --> 00:03:30.476
to close out the show.

00:03:30.476 --> 00:03:34.079
I appreciate that.
Thanks, everyone, for coming on.

00:03:34.079 --> 00:03:36.181
It's Tuesday, and we thought
it would be fun to do

00:03:36.181 --> 00:03:37.750
#AskTheFallons.

00:03:37.750 --> 00:03:40.419
This is where we ask you guys
to ask us questions,

00:03:40.419 --> 00:03:42.989
and we'll answer anything,
whether it be me, my kids,

00:03:42.989 --> 00:03:45.525
or my wife, who is also my
camera operator,

00:03:45.525 --> 00:03:47.726
who does not like
to be on-camera,

00:03:47.726 --> 00:03:48.861
but it doesn't matter.

00:03:48.861 --> 00:03:50.263
We're in a pandemic.

00:03:50.263 --> 00:03:52.398
We have to do things...
-That's right.

00:03:52.398 --> 00:03:53.699
-...that we don't want to do.

00:03:53.699 --> 00:03:55.368
So, here it is.

00:03:55.368 --> 00:03:57.169
It's time for another
installment of

00:03:57.169 --> 00:03:58.905
"Ask, Ask, Ask,
the, the, the,

00:03:58.905 --> 00:04:00.573
Fallons, Fallons, Fallons,
Fallons."

00:04:00.573 --> 00:04:06.546
♪♪

00:04:06.546 --> 00:04:07.780
Hi, guys.
-Hi.

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:10.817
-Welcome to this edition of
"Ask the Fallons."

00:04:10.817 --> 00:04:15.121
We are doing it from inside
the bus, the VW bus,

00:04:15.121 --> 00:04:17.856
because it is
a crazy storm outside.

00:04:17.856 --> 00:04:18.957
And we are safe
and cozy in here.

00:04:18.957 --> 00:04:21.360
-It might be hailing.

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:24.330
-And to be honest, the lighting
is just as good in here

00:04:24.330 --> 00:04:26.665
than it is outside.

00:04:26.665 --> 00:04:29.168
Alright, we got a lot of
questions from you guys.

00:04:29.168 --> 00:04:31.837
-Alright.
-Thank you so much.

00:04:31.837 --> 00:04:33.706
I am going to kind of talk
a little bit louder

00:04:33.706 --> 00:04:34.874
because it's just
really coming down.

00:04:34.874 --> 00:04:37.376
-It just --
It might be hailing right now.

00:04:37.376 --> 00:04:41.080
-Alright, here we go.
I think it is, actually, yeah.

00:04:41.080 --> 00:04:43.182
Alright, honey,
what were you like

00:04:43.182 --> 00:04:45.618
when you were the girls's age?

00:04:45.618 --> 00:04:46.886
So 5 and 6.

00:04:46.886 --> 00:04:50.589
-Okay, so, when I was 5 or 6 --
kindergarten --

00:04:50.589 --> 00:04:53.393
my best friend's name was Huey.

00:04:53.393 --> 00:04:57.663
And I loved to play and draw
and make-believe.

00:04:57.663 --> 00:04:59.832
I was really nice.

00:04:59.832 --> 00:05:05.071
I was very shy and I liked to be
kind of left alone.

00:05:05.071 --> 00:05:07.873
I had a younger brother who was
only about a year younger,

00:05:07.873 --> 00:05:12.745
so, yes, he would sort of,
you know, wait until

00:05:12.745 --> 00:05:16.915
I perfectly set up my entire
Barbie townhouse.

00:05:16.915 --> 00:05:18.184
-Was it Barbie
that you were into?

00:05:18.184 --> 00:05:20.719
-Oh, I was into Barbie.
I was into...

00:05:20.719 --> 00:05:23.856
-Skipper?
-Yeah, Skipper.

00:05:23.856 --> 00:05:27.660
And you could turn her arms,
and she would get bigger.

00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:30.762
She would turn into a pre-teen.
Oh, yeah.

00:05:30.762 --> 00:05:35.000
And, yeah, Jim would come in
and sort of mess it up,

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:37.002
and then I would go back
and fix it up again.

00:05:37.002 --> 00:05:41.473
And I was a peaceful child.
-You were. Very creative.

00:05:41.473 --> 00:05:42.675
-Very creative.

00:05:42.675 --> 00:05:48.247
I loved anything drawing,
painting, making a store.

00:05:48.247 --> 00:05:50.516
I made a lot of stores.
-Yeah.

00:05:50.516 --> 00:05:53.586
-That's still my dream
for one day, having a store.

00:05:53.586 --> 00:05:57.022
-For me, I think 5 years old,
5 and 6, was the first grade.

00:05:57.022 --> 00:05:59.191
I was very religious.

00:05:59.191 --> 00:06:02.227
I was very --
I wanted to be a priest.

00:06:02.227 --> 00:06:03.696
I was an altar boy.

00:06:03.696 --> 00:06:06.365
Just got my first communion,
and I was really into it.

00:06:06.365 --> 00:06:15.441
So I was very kind of religious
at the time when I was 5 and 6.

00:06:15.441 --> 00:06:17.509
I mean, I didn't have Barbies,
so I don't know.

00:06:17.509 --> 00:06:19.612
My sister had Barbies.

00:06:19.612 --> 00:06:21.780
We might have
Donny and Marie dolls.

00:06:21.780 --> 00:06:24.417
-Oh!
-So maybe I was into that.

00:06:24.417 --> 00:06:25.918
-We had the
Six Million Dollar Man.

00:06:25.918 --> 00:06:27.286
I remember I loved him.

00:06:27.286 --> 00:06:28.554
-No, I didn't have that.

00:06:28.554 --> 00:06:30.623
I remember I was jealous of
a lot of my friends.

00:06:30.623 --> 00:06:32.458
We'd go even to just
their garage sales

00:06:32.458 --> 00:06:34.126
and be like, "You have the
Evel Knievel bike

00:06:34.126 --> 00:06:35.294
that you pull the thing?"

00:06:35.294 --> 00:06:39.031
-For a quarter?
-Yes! Like, "I want that."

00:06:39.031 --> 00:06:40.332
We never had
any of those things.

00:06:40.332 --> 00:06:46.238
But I think that I
was probably more like --

00:06:46.238 --> 00:06:48.407
I think I was more like Winnie
than I was Franny.

00:06:48.407 --> 00:06:49.675
-You do?

00:06:49.675 --> 00:06:51.677
-Yeah, I think I was more quiet
and just, like,

00:06:51.677 --> 00:06:53.479
just did my own thing, yeah.

00:06:53.479 --> 00:06:56.281
My sister was -- Yeah,
she was more of the, like --

00:06:56.281 --> 00:06:58.317
-I'm surprised.

00:06:58.317 --> 00:07:00.419
-Everyone was really like,
"Oh, Gloria's --"

00:07:00.419 --> 00:07:02.254
-Oh, yeah, Gloria
was kind of a --

00:07:02.254 --> 00:07:04.657
-She was very cute
and very smart.

00:07:04.657 --> 00:07:06.058
-Yes.

00:07:06.058 --> 00:07:08.694
-And people saw that and they
were like, "Ooh, the boys.

00:07:08.694 --> 00:07:10.595
You're gonna have --"
-Good luck with the boy.

00:07:10.595 --> 00:07:14.467
-"You're gonna have
a little trouble with the boy."

00:07:14.467 --> 00:07:16.001
-But the daughter's really cute.

00:07:16.001 --> 00:07:18.804
-Yeah, she's cute and so smart.

00:07:18.804 --> 00:07:21.073
But he's got something, I guess.

00:07:21.073 --> 00:07:24.676
He's got something
to work towards.

00:07:24.676 --> 00:07:26.078
Hey, this one's good.

00:07:26.078 --> 00:07:28.681
It said, "I just started my
first serious relationship.

00:07:28.681 --> 00:07:31.617
Do you have any dating advice?"
-Oh!

00:07:31.617 --> 00:07:33.352
-Yeah, would you
like to go ahead?

00:07:33.352 --> 00:07:35.288
Mine -- I would not.
I would not give you any advice.

00:07:35.288 --> 00:07:40.326
-What would be
your advice, actually?

00:07:40.326 --> 00:07:44.463
-I'm so bad.
What's my dating advice?

00:07:44.463 --> 00:07:47.933
Phew! Maybe realize that when

00:07:47.933 --> 00:07:49.802
you're first starting to date,

00:07:49.802 --> 00:07:54.239
that this might not be
the one, the one.

00:07:54.239 --> 00:07:57.576
Most likely, it's not the one.
-It could be the one.

00:07:57.576 --> 00:07:58.677
-It could be the one.

00:07:58.677 --> 00:08:01.313
I would say you're in the
5 percentile

00:08:01.313 --> 00:08:03.415
of that being "the one."

00:08:03.415 --> 00:08:05.751
-I don't even know where to
start with my advice.

00:08:05.751 --> 00:08:08.187
Alright, I'll try to
limit it down...

00:08:08.187 --> 00:08:09.521
-Mm-hmm.

00:08:09.521 --> 00:08:12.558
-...to a big one, which is

00:08:12.558 --> 00:08:16.662
trust their actions,
not their words.

00:08:16.662 --> 00:08:19.932
So, you may hear
the most beautiful things,

00:08:19.932 --> 00:08:22.601
you may hear promises,
you might hear everything

00:08:22.601 --> 00:08:25.704
you want to hear, but if that
person you're dating

00:08:25.704 --> 00:08:29.074
is not following
up with their actions,

00:08:29.074 --> 00:08:30.842
then they're just words.

00:08:30.842 --> 00:08:32.411
So trust yourself on that.

00:08:32.411 --> 00:08:34.346
Stop saying, "But, but, but..."

00:08:34.346 --> 00:08:36.214
"But he said he liked me."

00:08:36.214 --> 00:08:39.618
If he doesn't show you
he liked you, you know,

00:08:39.618 --> 00:08:42.521
it might be a different thing.

00:08:42.521 --> 00:08:44.423
The other thing is
to know what you want.

00:08:44.423 --> 00:08:45.858
It's so important to know
what you want,

00:08:45.858 --> 00:08:49.628
because I think that a lot of us
just scream forward

00:08:49.628 --> 00:08:51.764
waiting for the first person
to pull out

00:08:51.764 --> 00:08:54.367
or pull up or, you know,
move ahead.

00:08:54.367 --> 00:08:55.568
And just know what you want.

00:08:55.568 --> 00:08:58.003
Some people might just
want to date,

00:08:58.003 --> 00:09:00.406
and you don't have to
rush to the altar.

00:09:00.406 --> 00:09:02.441
You don't have to
rush to anything.

00:09:02.441 --> 00:09:04.076
You can just enjoy each other.

00:09:04.076 --> 00:09:05.678
-Did you know
what you wanted with me?

00:09:05.678 --> 00:09:08.046
[ Both laugh ]
-Yes.

00:09:08.046 --> 00:09:09.348
The craziest thing about you
was that...

00:09:09.348 --> 00:09:10.382
-You wanted to run to the altar.

00:09:10.382 --> 00:09:12.218
-...for the first time
in my life,

00:09:12.218 --> 00:09:15.521
someone told me to make that
list of what I want.

00:09:15.521 --> 00:09:17.990
And I had just assumed that
I knew what I wanted,

00:09:17.990 --> 00:09:21.293
but I never actually
dated anyone

00:09:21.293 --> 00:09:25.764
who had anything on the
list of what I wanted

00:09:25.764 --> 00:09:27.099
once I wrote it down.

00:09:27.099 --> 00:09:30.002
So as soon as I did that --
and I'm not kidding --

00:09:30.002 --> 00:09:33.439
I went on to "Fever Pitch"
and ta-da.

00:09:33.439 --> 00:09:36.008
-Wow. So maybe write it down.
-Yeah.

00:09:36.008 --> 00:09:39.345
It's just a way of understanding
what you're after better.

00:09:39.345 --> 00:09:42.548
-You wanted
brown hair, good looks.

00:09:42.548 --> 00:09:46.985
-It was an entirely
physical list.

00:09:46.985 --> 00:09:47.986
-Physique.
-No, it was not.

00:09:47.986 --> 00:09:49.955
It had nothing to do with that.

00:09:49.955 --> 00:09:52.391
My 20-something self
would have made that list,

00:09:52.391 --> 00:09:56.662
but my older list was different.

00:09:56.662 --> 00:09:58.163
-But how different was it?

00:09:58.163 --> 00:10:00.799
-It was just more about, like,
someone who loves family,

00:10:00.799 --> 00:10:03.268
because some
people -- And I've dated people

00:10:03.268 --> 00:10:05.503
who don't actually want
to be around family,

00:10:05.503 --> 00:10:07.272
and I love my family.

00:10:07.272 --> 00:10:10.009
And, you know,
that was a big one.

00:10:10.009 --> 00:10:16.916
Someone who just had the same
sort of sense of fun that I did.

00:10:16.916 --> 00:10:18.183
-Yeah, and?

00:10:18.183 --> 00:10:22.354
-Someone who is really
muscly and great-looking!

00:10:22.354 --> 00:10:23.622
And the most handsome prince
in the world, my husband.

00:10:23.622 --> 00:10:28.026
-Gosh, I spend about 90%
of my day in the gym,

00:10:28.026 --> 00:10:30.596
and when I come out, you know
I got to have my protein shake.

00:10:30.596 --> 00:10:33.165
-Yeah, actually,
on the list is someone

00:10:33.165 --> 00:10:35.467
who isn't that into all of that,

00:10:35.467 --> 00:10:40.739
because that is like a widow
waiting to happen, a gym widow.

00:10:40.739 --> 00:10:43.309
-I mean, I just -- When I get in
there, I get pumped, man.

00:10:43.309 --> 00:10:45.911
I want to come out.
You know what I want?

00:10:45.911 --> 00:10:49.749
That protein shake and then
another round in the gym.

00:10:49.749 --> 00:10:51.350
-That was your list?
-That's all I wanted.

00:10:51.350 --> 00:10:55.821
-So you must be
gravely disappointed.

00:10:55.821 --> 00:10:58.557
-I think everyone knows,
at home, how lucky I am.

00:10:58.557 --> 00:11:01.326
[ Both laugh ]

00:11:01.326 --> 00:11:03.562
Alright, let's continue
with the show, everybody.

00:11:03.562 --> 00:11:05.864
-Okay.
-That was "Ask the Fallons."

00:11:05.864 --> 00:11:07.733
-Stay dry.

00:11:07.733 --> 00:11:09.434
-Thank you so much
for those questions.

00:11:09.434 --> 00:11:14.306
And thank you, VW, for inventing
that bus in the '70s.

00:11:14.306 --> 00:11:16.875
That thing is awesome,
and it kept us --

00:11:16.875 --> 00:11:22.415
It kept that bit going during
this rainy, haily, cold storm

00:11:22.415 --> 00:11:25.750
that was happening outside.

00:11:25.750 --> 00:11:28.186
What's up, bud?

00:11:28.186 --> 00:11:30.722
-Hi.
-What do you want to tell me?

00:11:30.722 --> 00:11:31.991
-You know what?
-What?

00:11:31.991 --> 00:11:35.761
-And do you know what?
-What? You always whisper.

00:11:35.761 --> 00:11:40.032
-[ Whispering ]
-Yeah, what?

00:11:40.032 --> 00:11:42.868
You are trying to be
"Bomb Boyeye."

00:11:42.868 --> 00:11:44.469
It's Bomb Voyage,
in "The Incredibles."

00:11:44.469 --> 00:11:47.306
She calls him Bomb Voyeye.

00:11:47.306 --> 00:11:49.642
So, anyways, thank you
for sending in those

00:11:49.642 --> 00:11:51.676
"Ask the Fallons" questions.

00:11:51.676 --> 00:11:55.113
And I appreciate them getting
a little bit deeper and --

00:11:55.113 --> 00:11:57.983
Wow, you guys are really asking
these hard-hitting questions.

00:11:57.983 --> 00:11:59.118
But, anyways, I appreciate it.

